#!/usr/bin/env bash
## =============================================================================
## deploy/postgres/backup.sh.template — nightly database backup (and how to restore)
## =============================================================================
## GUIDE STEP   12.4
## PURPOSE      A shell script, run every night by cron (the Linux scheduler)
##              on the server, that:
##              1. dumps the database with pg_dump into one file;
##              2. copies the file to OFF-SITE storage (another location, so a
##                 lost server does not take the backups with it);
##              3. deletes local dumps older than the retention period.
## USED BY      cron on the production server, e.g. the crontab line
##                15 2 * * * /opt/te/deploy/postgres/backup.sh >> /var/log/te-backup.log 2>&1
##              (every day at 02:15).
## DEPENDS ON   Docker Compose (pg_dump runs INSIDE the postgres container, so
##              its version always matches the server), an off-site target
##              (e.g. university storage via rclone or rsync).
## WHY          Guide 12.4: "A backup you have never restored is not a
##              backup." The restore drill below is part of this file.
## ACTIVATE     Copy to backup.sh, uncomment, fill in the off-site command,
##              `chmod 700 backup.sh` (only the owner may read or run it), add
##              the crontab line, then perform the restore drill once.
##              Line 1 (the shebang, which tells the system to run this file
##              with bash) is already active: a shebang works only on line 1.
##              Check the syntax with `bash -n backup.sh`.
## =============================================================================

